/*****************************************************************************
    cursorReadT -- HOW A MEMBER'S TAG IS READ FROM INSIDE AN ITERATE BODY,
    and the two spellings do not agree.

    Minted 2026-09-10 as stroke 6b's PRE-MEASURE. The `.`-is-binary ruling
    retires the leading/bare accessor form and says a collision between a bare
    name and a member is spelled explicitly, btCur.taG. This file measures both
    spellings before any of that is built, because a ruling that retires the
    working form in favour of a broken one is a ruling that goes dark.

    ARM A, no collision. Bare wins and the explicit form does not.
    ARM B, a field literally named taG declared in the enclosing define block.

    Both arms are needed: A alone cannot show the shadowing, and B alone cannot
    show that the bare form is the one that works when nothing shadows it.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(CursorRead);
define
    crBag   crAlpha crBeta;
    crN;
    taG="ENCLOSING";
    crWalk code={
        iterate crCur on *argument;
        while ++crCur;
            ++crN;
            cerr "CR-B" crN " bare=" taG " explicit=" crCur.taG:;
        };
    crRun code={
        crN = 0;
        crWalk(crBag);
        cerr "CR-B walked = " crN " (want 2 -- the anti-vacuity control)":;
        };
    ;
crRun();
print "CURSORREADB SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();
